        en: `## Vasily Shukshin (1929–1974)

Born in Srostki in the Altai, a village boy who became a film actor and director in Moscow («Калина красная», 1974, where he played the ex-convict Yegor Prokudin) and wrote about a hundred and twenty short stories about the people he came from. He died at forty-five during the filming of «Они сражались за Родину».

His hero is the **чудик**, the odd man of the village, who does everything wrong by the city's rules and right by his own heart.

| Story | Year | What happens | What it shows |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| «Чудик» | 1967 | Vasily Knyazev finds a fifty-rouble note in a shop, is ashamed to claim it, flies to his brother in the Urals, paints the baby's pram with flowers, and is thrown out by his brother's wife | the kind man who is a nuisance to everyone |
| «Срезал» | 1970 | Gleb Kapustin, the village's self-appointed intellectual, «cuts down» a visiting candidate of sciences with nonsense questions, and the village admires and fears him | envy and the hunger for respect |
| «Микроскоп» | 1969 | the carpenter Andrei Yerin tells his wife he lost 120 roubles, buys a microscope, studies microbes at night with his son and dreams of saving mankind; the wife sells the microscope | the dream of knowledge in a man nobody takes seriously |
| «Сапожки» | 1970 | Sergei Dukhanin buys his wife expensive boots that do not fit; she tries them on and the marriage is warm again for an evening | tenderness that has no words |
| «Обида» | 1971 | Sashka Yermolaev is insulted by a shop assistant and cannot let it go | the wounded dignity of the small man |

Shukshin's form is the **short story as a scene**: one event, a conversation, almost no description, the ending open. His language is the villagers' speech, with the narrator standing very close to the hero. The question under all the stories: what is a human being worth who is of no use to the state?

## Valentin Rasputin (1937–2015)

Born on the Angara in the Irkutsk region, Rasputin wrote four tales that are the summit of the village prose: «Последний срок» (1970), «Живи и помни» (1974), «Прощание с Матёрой» (1976), «Пожар» (1985), and the story «Уроки французского» (1973).

### «Прощание с Матёрой»

Matyora is an island village on the Angara, three hundred years old, to be flooded by the reservoir of the new dam. The old woman **Darya Pinigina** watches the last summer: the sanitary brigade comes to remove the crosses from the cemetery and the old women drive them off; Petrukha burns his own hut for the compensation; Darya's son Pavel is torn between the village and the new settlement; the young leave without regret. The island has a **Хозяин**, a small animal spirit nobody sees, and a **царский листвень**, the royal larch that the workers cannot cut down or burn. Darya whitewashes her hut before it is burned, as for a holiday. In the last chapter the men sent to bring out the old women are lost in fog on the river, and the book ends in fog, with the howl of the Master and the voice of Bogodul. Rasputin asks what a people does to itself when it floods its graves for electricity; Darya's answer is that «правда в памяти. У кого нет памяти, нет жизни».

### «Живи и помни»

In the last winter of the war the soldier **Andrei Guskov** deserts after a hospital and hides near his village; only his wife **Nastyona** knows, brings him food and becomes pregnant by him. She cannot tell the truth and cannot bear the lie; when the village begins to watch her, she drowns herself in the Angara. The title is the sentence passed on Andrei: live, and remember what you did to her.

### «Уроки французского»

An eleven-year-old boy from the village studies in the district town in 1948, half-starved; he plays чика for money to buy milk and is beaten. His French teacher, **Lidia Mikhailovna**, gives him extra lessons at home, sends him a parcel of macaroni he refuses, and finally plays a children's game for money with him so that he can win honestly. The headmaster finds them, and she leaves for her Kuban; a parcel arrives with macaroni and three red apples the boy has only seen in pictures. The lesson is not French: it is that help may have to disguise itself to be accepted.

## The two writers together

Shukshin's village is funny and sad, Rasputin's is tragic. Both wrote the moment when the Russian village stopped being the country's home, and both put the moral centre in a person the modern world had no use for: the odd man, the old woman, the deserter's wife, the teacher.

### Exercises

1. What does the «чудик» do in his brother's house, and why is he thrown out?
2. What does Gleb Kapustin want in «Срезал»?
3. Why does Yerin's wife sell the microscope, and what does the story mourn?
4. Why does Matyora have to disappear?
5. What is the royal larch, and what happens when they try to fell it?
6. Why does Nastyona drown herself?
7. Why does Lidia Mikhailovna play for money with her pupil?

### Answers

He paints the baby's pram with flowers and birds to please his brother's wife, who sees it as spoiling a thing worth money and as another proof that a village fool has come to stay. He wants to be respected and feared as the cleverest man of the village, and he gets that by humiliating a guest who has achieved what he never could. She sells it because 120 roubles is a month's bread and the microscope is a toy; the story mourns the dream of knowledge and wonder in a man whose life allows neither. The island will be flooded by the reservoir of the new hydroelectric dam on the Angara. It is the ancient larch that stands for the island's roots; axes, saws and fire cannot bring it down, and the workers leave it standing. She is pregnant by her husband, a deserter whom she cannot betray and whose crime she cannot confess, and when the village begins to watch her she has no road left. He is starving and proud, refuses her parcel, and the only way she can give him money he will take is to let him win it in a game.`,
